<!-- Website-only teaching for android/performance/memory-management-and-leaks.md; original chapter remains read-only. Sources checked 2026-10-25. -->

<!-- @opening -->

<p class="story-lead">Open a profile, go back, repeat. The old screens should be irrelevant, yet a listener registry still points to each one. Memory growth is now an ownership problem: who promised to remove those references?</p>

Follow a strong reference path from a live owner to an object whose useful lifetime ended, then repair that registration boundary.

<!-- @after Internal Mechanics :: visual -->

### One retained edge keeps a larger graph alive

<figure class="viz"><div class="viz-flow" style="--cols:3"><div class="node"><strong>Long-lived registry</strong><small>Still reachable during this app session.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Registered listener</strong><small>Captures the old screen or its view.</small></div><div class="node"><strong>Destroyed screen graph</strong><small>No longer useful, but retained through that path.</small></div></div><figcaption>Object count and retained size answer different questions: one small listener can retain a much larger object graph.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Production Example :: animation -->

### A registration outlives its screen

<figure class="stepper" data-illustrated data-no-handoff><span class="stepper-title">A registration outlives its screen</span><div class="stage viz-grid" style="--cols:2"><div class="node" data-el="registry" data-art="memory" data-art-role="storage"><strong>Registry</strong><small>Long-lived owner</small></div><div class="node" data-el="listener" data-art="code" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Listener</strong><small>Captures screen</small></div><div class="node" data-el="screen" data-art="phone" data-art-role="client"><strong>Screen</strong><small>Useful while visible</small></div><div class="node" data-el="heap" data-art="inspect" data-art-role="runtime"><strong>Heap evidence</strong><small>Retaining reference path</small></div></div><script type="application/json" class="steps">[{"label": "Register", "caption": "The screen registers a callback. A strong path from the registry to the screen is legitimate while that ownership relationship is needed.", "state": {"registry": "active", "listener": "active", "screen": "active"}, "add": ["registry", "listener", "screen", "heap"]}, {"label": "Leave", "caption": "The screen’s useful lifetime ends, but the registration remains. The old object is still strongly reachable through the listener.", "state": {"screen": "warn"}}, {"label": "Investigate", "caption": "Repeat navigation and inspect retained objects and the reference path. A rising heap alone does not identify which edge is wrong.", "state": {"heap": "active"}}, {"label": "Remove the stale edge", "caption": "Match deregistration to the owner’s actual lifecycle, then repeat the scenario and inspect whether the old path remains.", "state": {"listener": "ghost", "heap": "done"}}]</script><figcaption>Illustrative sequence. Use Previous, Next or Play; every step is also available as text.</figcaption></figure>

<!-- @after Staff Engineer Thinking :: staff -->

Repeat the navigation loop that grows memory, then distinguish retained objects from short-lived allocation churn. For a retained screen, trace the strong-reference path back to its owner and define when that registration, callback or cache entry ends. Verify release across repeated visits, not just one garbage collection. If the process still disappears after managed retention is fixed, inspect native/graphics usage and system pressure rather than declaring every kill a Java heap leak.

<!-- @before Further Reading :: check -->

### Check your diagnosis

<details><summary>Can one listener retain more memory than its own shallow size?</summary>

Yes. Objects reachable through that listener contribute to the retained graph.

</details>

<details><summary>Does memory growth alone prove an Activity leak?</summary>

No. Check retention paths, allocation churn, caches and other process-memory buckets.

</details>

<details><summary>What validates the registration fix?</summary>

Repeat the same lifecycle transitions and inspect whether the stale path and retained instances remain.

</details>

<!-- @before Further Reading :: deeper -->

